  "worked_solution": "To calculate the probability of an event using a Venn diagram, we count the number of outcomes in the region that represents the event, and divide by the total number of outcomes of the sample space $S.$\n\nIn the Venn diagram, $A\\cap B$ is represented by the area that is inside the overlap between the circles (see the illustration below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14242110/assets/c0c7df273c9b71fc0286b03d7405cb8ca473d14b1020fc1ec15530879ddb8f1c.png)\n\nWe count up the outcomes:\n\n- The number of outcomes in $A\\cap B$ is $15.$\n\n- The total number of outcomes in $S$ is\n\n$$\n20+15+25+40=100.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(A\\cap B)=\\frac{15}{100}=\\frac{3}{20}=0.15\n$$\n\nto $2$ decimal places.",
